    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Google&#8217;s latest Search upgrade promises better answers to your most complicated questions",
      "d": "Powered by Gemini 4 Hydrogen, Google's AI Mode is getting better at understanding complex queries, surfacing relevant links, and delivering richer visual responses.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/12/googles-latest-search-upgrade-promises-better-answers-to-your-most-complicated-questions/",
      "m": "11. 12. PM 07:27",
      "ts": 1794511665000,
      "fullText": "Google Search is getting better at understanding complicated questions, including those with multiple parts, highly specific requests, or confusing typos. The updates are coming via the company&#8217;s newly released Gemini 4 Hydrogen model, which Google claims is its &#8220;biggest model improvement to Search yet.&#8221; While Google&#8217;s frontier models, Gemini 4 Carbon and Gemini 4 Argon, are best at deep reasoning for hard problems, Hydrogen is designed for fast execution across a variety of agentic workflows, or tasks in which an AI carries out multiple steps on its own, the company explained in an announcement. The idea behind the model&#8217;s release is that using the most powerful model isn&#8217;t always needed &#8212; sometimes a faster, cheaper, and reasonably smart one will do. Google says Hydrogen completes tasks 2.5 times faster than Gemini 3.8 Flash, its previous fast model, but it&#8217;s still pretty smart, even beating the more powerful Gemini 4 Argon on one broad intelligence benchmark, the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index , at its default high thinking level. In Search, Hydrogen brings several improvements, including a better understanding of user intent, the ability to offer more helpful links and visual responses, and better answers across the different languages supported by Google&#8217;s AI Mode. In terms of intent, Google says Hydrogen can handle more nuanced and complicated questions more quickly. This means people can ask their questions in their natural train of thought, including those that have multiple parts or that include highly specific requests. Hydrogen is also better at navigating hard-to-understand typos, Google says. Because of its improvements and better ability to follow instructions and call the right tools, Hydrogen is able to more precisely address each part of a user&#8217;s question. Its advanced reasoning also improves Search&#8217;s fan-out technique, which breaks down a single user&#8217;s prompt into multi"
    },
